▪ My Mac-Book M5

▪▪ Overview

◦ Model: MacBook Pro
◦ Model Identifier: Mac17,6
◦ SoC: Apple M5 Max
◦ CPU: 18 Cores, consisting of 6 Super Cores and 12 Performance Cores
◦ CPU Core Count reported by macOS: 18
◦ GPU: 40 Cores, Metal Supported
◦ Neural Engine: 16 Cores according to the Apple MacBook Pro Technical Specifications
◦ Neural Accelerators: Present in the M5 Max SoC according to Apple
◦ Unified Memory: 128 GB
◦ Nominal Unified-Memory Bandwidth: 614 GB/s for the 40-Core-GPU M5 Max
◦ Instruction Architecture: arm64
◦ Operating System: MacOS 26.5.1, Build 25F80

The CPU and GPU Core Counts were read locally with ⌜system_profiler⌟. The
Neural Engine Core Count and the Nominal Memory Bandwidth come from the Apple
Technical Specifications for the M5 Max Configuration with 40-Core GPU:
⌜https://www.apple.com/de/macbook-pro/specs/⌟. macOS does not expose the
Neural Engine as a selectable MLX Device in the local Probe.

The System Hardware Report also exposed a Serial Number, Hardware UUID and
Provisioning UDID. These Values are intentionally not copied into the
Experiment Documentation because they are not required for Runtime Comparison.

▪▪ Internal Storage

◦ Internal Fixed Solid-State Storage
◦ Capacity: 8.0 TB
◦ Filesystem: APFS
◦ Container Free Space: About 401.7 GB at the Observation-Time
◦ Mounted Data-Volume available: About 374 GiB at the Observation-Time
◦ Hardware AES-Support: Yes
◦ SMART Status: Verified

The Root-Volume was presented as a read-only APFS System-Snapshot. The
Filesystem Capacity and Free-Space Values are therefore recorded separately,
because the Container-View and the mounted Data-Volume View use different
Accounting-Boundaries.

